  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nWe will use a two-stage optimization approach with progressive grid refinement. The core idea is to represent the step function h using a compressed representation (discrete pulse locations and weights) rather than point-by-point optimization. This leverages the sparsity of optimal solutions in discrepancy problems. The approach consists of:\n- First optimizing at n=70 grid points using the parent solution\n- Then refining to n=100 grid points with an interpolation-based transformation\n- The optimization minimizes the maximum correlation by:\n  - Parametrizing h with piecewise constant segments (breaking points)\n  - Ensuring integral preservation and value bounds through explicit constraints\n  - Utilizing gradient-based local search to refine solutions found at coarser grids\n\nThis method addresses the high dimensionality of the problem by reducing parameters, avoids local optima through global perturbations, and ensures solution feasibility by maintaining integral preservation during grid refinement.\n\nKey limitations:\n- Requires careful handling of gradient computation at different grid sizes\n- Relys on interpolation which may not perfectly preserve function characteristics\n- Computation time increases with grid size due to O(n\u00b2) correlation operations\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nVariables:\n- h_{n}: Grid function at grid size n \u2208 {70, 100}\n- dx_{n} = 2/n grid spacing\n- x_i = i * dx_{n} spatial coordinates\n\nObjective to minimize:\nC\u2085(n) = dx_n * max_{k\u2208[-(n-1),n-1]} [h(x) * (1-h(x+k))] integrated\nComputed via:\n    corr = np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full')\n    C\u2085(n) = dx_n * max(corr)\n\nConstraints:\n- \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n- 0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x\n\nFor grid adaptation:\n- Transform h_{n}\u2192h_{m} via linear interpolation after grid adjustment\n- Scale and clip to maintain integral and bounds\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n   a. Parent initialization:\n        - Start with provided initial_h_values at n=70\n        - Check feasibility: sum(initial_h_values) \u2248 35 (with 70 grid points)\n\n   b. Optimization routine (SciPy minimize):\n        - Method=\"SLSQP\" for gradient-based optimization\n        - Objective: dx_n * np.max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full'))\n        - Constraint: {type:'eq', 'fun': lambda h: sum(h) - n/2}\n        - Bounds: [(0,1)]*n\n        - Initial guess: either parent solution or adapted grid interpolation\n\n   c. Grid transition (n=70 to n=100):\n        - Compute interpolation points: new indices 0:200, old 0:70\n        - Interpolate values with np.interp\n        - Adjust scale factor to maintain unit integral\n        - Clip values to [0,1] if necessary\n\n   d. Objective computation:\n        - Compute correlation using mode='full' \n        - Scale by dx_n for fair comparison across grids\n\n   e. Validation:\n        - Check h in [0,1] with np.all and np.max\n        - Verify sum(h) == n/2 (allow tolerance 0.01)\n\n   f. Termination conditions:\n        - Maximum time per grid: 500s (with budget_s=1100s split)\n        - Convergence: minimum change in objective < 1e-4 between iterations\n        - Best solution tracking across grids\n\n4. Search choices and variations:\n   - Grid sizes: [70, 100]\n   - Optimization parameters:\n        max_iter=500\n        tol=1e-4 for convergence\n        ftol=1e-4 for function improvement\n   - Grid transition:\n        interpolation_order=1 (linear)\n        scale_factor from interpolation before optimization\n   - If optimization stalls, employ restart with:\n        random_wiggle = np.random.uniform(-0.05, 0.05, n)\n        but preserve mean value\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness:\n- Dominant cost: O(n\u00b2) per correlation at grid size\n- Estimated time:\n    n=70: ~5s/eval serves fews\n- Resource utilization:\n    CPU-bound, use minimal memory (arrays of size n)\n- Failure handling:\n    - If objective evaluation invalid: clip and reevaluate\n    - If gradient estimation fails: use finite differences fallback\n    - Budget-based cut-off: return best known solution\n\n6. Validation and return contract:\n- Feasibility check:\n    0 <= h[i] <= 1 for all i\n    abs(sum(h) - n/2) < 0.01\n- Objective computation:\n    repeat using mode='full' to validate\n- Final solution:\n    h_final = adapted to working grid\n    c5_bound = minimal C\u2085 found\n    n_points = grid size used (100 if optimized)",
